// Imperative: verbose, says HOW to iterate.
List<String> hot = new ArrayList<>();
for (Order o : orders) {
    if (o.amount() > 100) hot.add(o.id().toUpperCase());
}

// Declarative stream: says WHAT to compute; library handles iteration.
List<String> hot = orders.stream()                  // source
    .filter(o -> o.amount() > 100)                   // intermediate (lazy, stateless)
    .map(o -> o.id().toUpperCase())                   // intermediate (lazy, stateless)
    .toList();                                       // terminal — triggers execution

// Laziness + fusion: nothing runs until .toList(). At execution, filter+map
//   are FUSED into one pass — no intermediate collection is materialized.

// Single-use: a Stream cannot be reused after a terminal op.
Stream<Order> s = orders.stream();
s.count();                       // OK
s.toList();                      // throws IllegalStateException — already consumed

// Side-effect bug: mutating external state in forEach (order non-deterministic).
//   DON'T:  list.forEach(x -> results.add(x))   // use collect instead.
//   DO:    results = list.stream().filter(...).collect(Collectors.toList());

A lambda is `(parameters) -> body`. Various legal forms:

```java
// No parameters
Runnable r = () -> System.out.println("hello");

// One parameter, single expression — parens optional, no return needed
Function<String, Integer> length = s -> s.length();

// Multiple parameters, single expression
BinaryOperator<Integer> sum = (a, b) -> a + b;

// Block body with explicit return
Function<User, String> describe = (user) -> {
    String name = user.getName();
    int age = user.getAge();
    return name + " (" + age + ")";
};

// Explicit parameter types (rarely needed; useful for disambiguation)
BiFunction<Long, String, User> create = (Long id, String name) -> new User(id, name);

// var in parameters (Java 11+) — useful for adding annotations
Predicate<String> nonEmpty = (var s) -> !s.isEmpty();
Predicate<String> annotated = (@NonNull var s) -> !s.isEmpty();
```

**Target typing** — the lambda's type comes from the context. The same expression `s -> s.length()` is `Function<String, Integer>` in one context, `ToIntFunction<String>` in another. The compiler looks at the expected type (variable type, parameter type, return type) and matches the lambda's signature to the **single abstract method** of that functional interface.

A lambda can read variables from the enclosing scope, but with rules:

**1. Local variables must be effectively final** — either declared `final` or never reassigned after initialization.
```java
String prefix = "ID-";  // effectively final
Function<Long, String> withPrefix = id -> prefix + id;  // OK

String prefix2 = "ID-";
prefix2 = "NUM-";  // reassigned
Function<Long, String> broken = id -> prefix2 + id;
// COMPILE ERROR: variable used in lambda must be effectively final
```
**Why**: lambdas may be invoked later, on a different thread; capturing by reference would create thread-safety issues and stale-state bugs. The compiler enforces capture-by-value semantics for clarity.

**2. Instance fields and static fields can be freely accessed and even mutated** (but mutation is a code smell):
```java
class Counter {
    int count = 0;

    Runnable increment = () -> count++;  // OK, count is a field
}
```

**3. Workaround for mutable capture** — use an array or `AtomicReference`/`AtomicInteger`:
```java
// Using AtomicInteger (preferred for thread-safe counters)
AtomicInteger counter = new AtomicInteger(0);
list.forEach(item -> counter.incrementAndGet());

// Using a one-element array (less idiomatic)
int[] counter2 = {0};
list.forEach(item -> counter2[0]++);
```
**Note**: this defeats the purpose of effectively-final and makes the lambda mutating shared state — in parallel streams it'd be unsafe.

**4. `this` is lexical** — always refers to the enclosing class instance, not the lambda:
```java
class Service {
    String name = "svc";
    Runnable action = () -> System.out.println(this.name);  // Service's `this`
    Runnable anonAction = new Runnable() {
        public void run() { System.out.println(this); }  // anonymous instance's `this`
    };
}
```

**Stream operations** — the most common use case:
```java
List<String> activeNames = users.stream()
    .filter(u -> u.isActive())
    .map(u -> u.getName())
    .toList();
```

**Comparator** — lambdas replace sprawling anonymous classes:
```java
list.sort((a, b) -> a.getName().compareTo(b.getName()));
// Even cleaner with method reference + Comparator combinator
list.sort(Comparator.comparing(User::getName));
```

**Event handlers**:
```java
button.setOnClickListener(event -> handleClick(event));
```

**Custom callbacks**:
```java
repository.findById(id, user -> processUser(user), () -> handleNotFound());
```

**Functional builders**:
```java
public User build(Consumer<UserBuilder> config) {
    UserBuilder builder = new UserBuilder();
    config.accept(builder);
    return builder.build();
}
User u = build(b -> b.name("Alice").email("alice@x.com"));
```

**Strategy pattern**:
```java
Map<String, Function<Order, BigDecimal>> taxStrategies = Map.of(
    "US", order -> order.getTotal().multiply(new BigDecimal("0.07")),
    "EU", order -> order.getTotal().multiply(new BigDecimal("0.20")),
    "NONE", order -> BigDecimal.ZERO);
```

**Lazy initialization with Supplier**:
```java
Map<String, User> cache = new HashMap<>();
User u = cache.computeIfAbsent(id, key -> repo.findById(key));
```

A **functional interface** has **exactly one abstract method**. That's the entire contract.

```java
@FunctionalInterface
interface Greeter {
    String greet(String name);  // one abstract method
}

Greeter formal = name -> "Good day, " + name;
Greeter casual = name -> "Hey, " + name;

System.out.println(formal.greet("Alice"));  // Good day, Alice
```

**What doesn't count toward the SAM**:
- `default` methods — they have an implementation.
- `static` methods — they belong to the interface itself.
- `private` methods (Java 9+) — helpers for default methods.
- Methods that override `Object` methods (`equals`, `hashCode`, `toString`).

```java
@FunctionalInterface
interface MyInterface {
    int apply(int x);                          // SAM

    default int applyTwice(int x) {            // doesn't count
        return apply(apply(x));
    }

    static MyInterface identity() {            // doesn't count
        return x -> x;
    }

    @Override
    boolean equals(Object obj);                // Object method, doesn't count
}
// Still a valid functional interface — only `apply` is abstract
```

**This is why `Comparator<T>` is a functional interface** even though it has many methods — only `compare(T, T)` is abstract; the rest are `default` (`reversed`, `thenComparing`) or `static` (`comparing`, `naturalOrder`).

`@FunctionalInterface` is a **compile-time check**. Adding it tells the compiler "this interface is intentionally functional; fail the build if anyone adds a second abstract method."

```java
@FunctionalInterface
interface Validator<T> {
    boolean isValid(T input);
    boolean alsoCheck(T input);  // COMPILE ERROR: not a functional interface
}
```

Without `@FunctionalInterface`, the compiler still allows the interface to be used with a lambda **if** it has exactly one abstract method. So technically the annotation is optional. But:
- It documents intent.
- It prevents future maintainers from breaking the contract by adding a method.
- It catches the bug at the interface definition (where the mistake happened) instead of at every lambda use site.

**Rule of thumb**: add `@FunctionalInterface` to any interface you design to be used with lambdas. Don't add it to interfaces that happen to have one abstract method but aren't meant to be functional (e.g., `Closeable`).

Use a standard one if it fits. Define a custom one only when:

**1. You need a checked exception**:
```java
@FunctionalInterface
interface ThrowingFunction<T, R> {
    R apply(T input) throws Exception;
}

ThrowingFunction<String, Path> toPath = s -> Files.createFile(Path.of(s));
```
Standard `Function<T, R>` doesn't declare any exception, so you'd have to wrap checked exceptions in lambdas — a common annoyance.

**2. You have more than two parameters**:
```java
@FunctionalInterface
interface TriFunction<T, U, V, R> {
    R apply(T t, U u, V v);
}

TriFunction<User, Order, Address, Receipt> generate =
    (user, order, address) -> new Receipt(user, order, address, Instant.now());
```
Standard library stops at `Bi*` (two parameters). For three-plus, define your own or use a record/tuple.

**3. The semantic name matters more than the structural type**:
```java
@FunctionalInterface
public interface RetryPolicy {
    boolean shouldRetry(int attempt, Throwable cause);
}
```
You could write `BiPredicate<Integer, Throwable>` but `RetryPolicy` documents the domain meaning at every use site.

**4. Domain-specific defaults are useful**:
```java
@FunctionalInterface
public interface RateLimiter {
    boolean tryAcquire();

    default boolean tryAcquire(int permits) {
        for (int i = 0; i < permits; i++) {
            if (!tryAcquire()) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

**Don't define a custom interface** when a standard one works — it's just clutter.

Functional interfaces aren't limited to one method overall — only one abstract method. Default and static methods make them composable and self-documenting.

**Comparator** is the classic example:
```java
Comparator<User> byName = Comparator.comparing(User::getName);              // static
Comparator<User> byNameThenAge = byName.thenComparing(User::getAge);       // default
Comparator<User> reversed = byName.reversed();                              // default
Comparator<User> nullsFirst = Comparator.nullsFirst(byName);               // static
```

**Function** combinators:
```java
Function<String, String> trim = String::trim;
Function<String, Integer> length = String::length;
Function<String, Integer> trimLength = trim.andThen(length);
Function<String, String> identity = Function.identity();  // static
```

**Predicate** combinators:
```java
Predicate<String> notEmpty = Predicate.not(String::isEmpty);  // Java 11+
Predicate<String> shortAndNotEmpty = notEmpty.and(s -> s.length() < 10);
```

**Custom interface with defaults**:
```java
@FunctionalInterface
public interface JsonSerializer<T> {
    String serialize(T input);

    default JsonSerializer<T> withPrefix(String prefix) {
        return input -> prefix + serialize(input);
    }

    default String serializeAll(Collection<T> items) {
        return items.stream().map(this::serialize).collect(Collectors.joining(",", "[", "]"));
    }
}
```
Clients still implement only `serialize` via lambda, but get the convenience methods for free.

Before Java 8, to pass 'a piece of behavior' to a method you had to wrap it in an anonymous class:

```java
Collections.sort(list, new Comparator<String>() {
    @Override
    public int compare(String a, String b) {
        return a.length() - b.length();
    }
});
```

This worked but was verbose — too much syntax for 'compare by length'. Java 8's lambdas eliminated the noise:

```java
Collections.sort(list, (a, b) -> a.length() - b.length());
```

Lambdas + the standard functional interfaces + the Stream API together changed how Java code is written. Collections processing became pipelines; event handlers became one-liners; concurrent programming got `CompletableFuture` callbacks.

**Functional interface**: any interface with exactly one abstract method. The `@FunctionalInterface` annotation is a compile-time check — it fails to compile if a second abstract method is added. It's not required (any single-abstract-method interface qualifies), but it's best practice for interfaces intended as lambda targets.

**Standard functional interfaces** live in `java.util.function` and cover the common shapes:

- Take one argument, return something → **`Function<T, R>`**, or specializations
- Take one argument, return boolean → **`Predicate<T>`**
- Take one argument, return nothing → **`Consumer<T>`**
- Take nothing, return something → **`Supplier<T>`**
- Same-type transformations → **`UnaryOperator<T>`**, **`BinaryOperator<T>`**

For two-argument versions, prefix with `Bi-`: `BiFunction`, `BiConsumer`, `BiPredicate`. For primitives, suffix or prefix: `IntFunction<R>`, `ToIntFunction<T>`, `IntPredicate`, `IntToLongFunction`. The primitive variants exist because boxing `int → Integer` in a hot loop is measurable overhead — avoid it when you can.

```java
import java.util.function.*;

// Basic lambdas for each standard interface
Function<String, Integer> length = s -> s.length();
Predicate<Integer> positive = i -> i > 0;
Consumer<String> logger = System.out::println;
Supplier<List<String>> emptyList = ArrayList::new;
BiFunction<Integer, Integer, Integer> add = Integer::sum;

// COMPOSITION — default methods on functional interfaces

// Function.andThen: apply first, then transform result
Function<String, Integer> lengthPlusOne = length.andThen(i -> i + 1);
lengthPlusOne.apply("hi");  // 3

// Function.compose: apply argument first, then this
Function<String, Integer> trimmedLength = length.compose(String::trim);
trimmedLength.apply("  hi  ");  // 2

// Predicate combinators
Predicate<String> nonNull = Objects::nonNull;
Predicate<String> nonEmpty = s -> !s.isEmpty();
Predicate<String> valid = nonNull.and(nonEmpty);
Predicate<String> invalid = valid.negate();
Predicate<String> nullOrLong = nonNull.negate().or(s -> s.length() > 100);

// Consumer.andThen: chain side effects
Consumer<String> logAndProcess = logger.andThen(this::process);

// THREE-PART LAMBDA SYNTAX
// 1. Expression body — implicit return
Function<Integer, Integer> square = x -> x * x;

// 2. Block body — explicit return
Function<Integer, Integer> abs = x -> {
    if (x < 0) return -x;
    return x;
};

// 3. No-argument lambda
Supplier<Long> now = () -> System.currentTimeMillis();

// TYPE INFERENCE — let the compiler figure out types
BiFunction<Integer, Integer, Integer> add2 = (a, b) -> a + b;  // inferred Integer
// Explicit only when ambiguous or for documentation
BiFunction<Integer, Integer, Integer> add3 = (Integer a, Integer b) -> a + b;
```

**Primitive specializations in a hot loop**:

```java
int[] values = new int[1_000_000];
// ... populate

// BAD — boxes each int to Integer
long sum = IntStream.range(0, values.length)
    .boxed()
    .mapToInt(Integer::intValue)  // unbox again
    .sum();

// GOOD — stays in int throughout
IntUnaryOperator doubled = x -> x * 2;
int sum2 = Arrays.stream(values)
    .map(doubled)
    .sum();
```

**Signature**: `R apply(T t)` — takes T, returns R.

```java
Function<String, Integer> length = String::length;
Function<User, String> name = User::getName;
Function<Order, BigDecimal> total = Order::getTotal;

// In stream
List<String> names = users.stream().map(name).toList();

// Composition with andThen — apply this, then the next
Function<String, String> trim = String::trim;
Function<String, String> upper = String::toUpperCase;
Function<String, String> trimUpper = trim.andThen(upper);
trimUpper.apply("  hello  ");  // "HELLO"

// Composition with compose — apply the next first, then this
Function<String, String> upperTrim = trim.compose(upper);  // upper then trim

// Identity function (useful in toMap)
Map<Long, User> byId = users.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(User::getId, Function.identity()));
```

**Variants**:
- `BiFunction<T, U, R>` — two inputs.
- `UnaryOperator<T>` extends `Function<T, T>` — same input/output type.
- `BinaryOperator<T>` extends `BiFunction<T, T, T>` — used in `reduce`.
- Primitive: `IntFunction<R>`, `ToIntFunction<T>`, `LongFunction<R>`, `DoubleFunction<R>`, `IntToLongFunction`, etc.

**Avoid boxing**:
```java
// Boxed — each int becomes Integer
Function<Integer, Integer> doubleIt = i -> i * 2;

// Primitive — no boxing
IntUnaryOperator doubleIt2 = i -> i * 2;
int result = doubleIt2.applyAsInt(5);  // 10
```

**Real-world example — strategy pattern**:
```java
Map<String, Function<Order, BigDecimal>> taxStrategies = Map.of(
    "US", o -> o.getTotal().multiply(new BigDecimal("0.07")),
    "EU", o -> o.getTotal().multiply(new BigDecimal("0.20")),
    "NONE", o -> BigDecimal.ZERO
);
BigDecimal tax = taxStrategies.getOrDefault(country, o -> BigDecimal.ZERO).apply(order);
```

**Signature**: `boolean test(T t)` — takes T, returns boolean.

```java
Predicate<String> notEmpty = s -> !s.isEmpty();
Predicate<String> startsWithA = s -> s.startsWith("A");
Predicate<User> isActive = User::isActive;
Predicate<User> isAdmin = u -> u.getRole() == Role.ADMIN;

// In stream
List<User> activeUsers = users.stream().filter(isActive).toList();
long adminCount = users.stream().filter(isAdmin).count();
boolean anyAdmin = users.stream().anyMatch(isAdmin);
boolean allActive = users.stream().allMatch(isActive);
boolean noneInactive = users.stream().noneMatch(isActive.negate());

// Combinators
Predicate<User> activeAdmin = isActive.and(isAdmin);
Predicate<User> activeOrAdmin = isActive.or(isAdmin);
Predicate<User> notAdmin = isAdmin.negate();

// Java 11+: Predicate.not — cleaner than method-reference negation
List<String> nonEmptyLines = lines.stream()
    .filter(Predicate.not(String::isBlank))
    .toList();
// vs the awkward: .filter(s -> !s.isBlank())

// Static helpers
Predicate<User> nullSafe = Predicate.isEqual(targetUser);  // null-safe equals
```

**Variants**:
- `BiPredicate<T, U>` — two inputs.
- Primitive: `IntPredicate`, `LongPredicate`, `DoublePredicate`.

**Composing complex predicates**:
```java
Predicate<Order> isHighValue = o -> o.getTotal().compareTo(new BigDecimal("1000")) > 0;
Predicate<Order> isRecent = o -> o.getDate().isAfter(LocalDate.now().minusDays(30));
Predicate<Order> needsReview = isHighValue.and(isRecent.or(o -> o.isFlagged()));
```

**Signature**: `void accept(T t)` — takes T, returns nothing.

```java
Consumer<String> print = System.out::println;
Consumer<User> log = user -> logger.info("Processing user: {}", user.getName());
Consumer<Order> save = orderRepo::save;

// In stream
users.forEach(print);
orders.stream().filter(Order::isPending).forEach(save);

// Composition with andThen — apply this, then the next
Consumer<User> logAndEmail = log.andThen(emailService::sendWelcome);
users.forEach(logAndEmail);

// peek — Consumer for debugging in pipelines
List<String> result = users.stream()
    .peek(u -> System.out.println("Before filter: " + u))
    .filter(User::isActive)
    .peek(u -> System.out.println("After filter: " + u))
    .map(User::getName)
    .toList();
```

**Variants**:
- `BiConsumer<T, U>` — two inputs (e.g., `Map.forEach((k, v) -> ...)`).
- Primitive: `IntConsumer`, `LongConsumer`, `DoubleConsumer`.
- `ObjIntConsumer<T>`, `ObjLongConsumer<T>`, `ObjDoubleConsumer<T>` — mixed.

**Real-world — functional builder**:
```java
public User build(Consumer<UserBuilder> config) {
    UserBuilder builder = new UserBuilder();
    config.accept(builder);
    return builder.build();
}
User u = build(b -> b.name("Alice").email("alice@x.com").role(Role.ADMIN));
```

**Don't abuse**:
- `Consumer` is for side effects; if you have to write one in a stream, that's often a code smell.
- Prefer `map` + `collect` for transformation; `forEach` is for terminal side effects only.

**Signature**: `T get()` — takes nothing, returns T.

```java
Supplier<List<String>> emptyList = ArrayList::new;
Supplier<UUID> idGen = UUID::randomUUID;
Supplier<Instant> now = Instant::now;

// Lazy default — only computed if value is missing
User user = userRepo.findById(id)
    .orElseGet(() -> createGuestUser());  // Supplier, not eagerly called

String config = optionalConfig.orElseGet(this::loadDefaultConfig);

// Map.computeIfAbsent — also takes a supplier-like Function
cache.computeIfAbsent(key, k -> expensiveLookup(k));

// Stream.generate — infinite stream from supplier
Stream<Integer> randoms = Stream.generate(() -> ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextInt());
List<Integer> tenRandoms = randoms.limit(10).toList();

// Lazy logging (avoid expensive string construction if log level off)
logger.debug("Heavy computation result: {}", () -> heavyComputation());
```

**Variants**:
- Primitive: `IntSupplier`, `LongSupplier`, `DoubleSupplier`, `BooleanSupplier`.

**`orElse` vs `orElseGet` — the eager/lazy trap**:
```java
String result = optional.orElse(expensiveDefault());
// Calls expensiveDefault() ALWAYS, even if optional is present

String result2 = optional.orElseGet(() -> expensiveDefault());
// Calls only if optional is empty — use this for expensive defaults
```

**Real-world — Spring's `@Lazy` and `Provider`**:
- Spring's `Provider<T>` is a `Supplier<T>` for late binding.
- Lazy initialization patterns rely on `Supplier` to defer construction.

**Two-argument variants** — same shape, takes two inputs:
```java
BiFunction<String, String, String> concat = (a, b) -> a + b;
BiPredicate<String, Integer> longerThan = (s, n) -> s.length() > n;
BiConsumer<String, Integer> printPair = (s, n) -> System.out.println(s + "=" + n);

// Map.forEach uses BiConsumer
map.forEach((k, v) -> System.out.println(k + " → " + v));

// reduce with BinaryOperator (specialized BiFunction<T, T, T>)
int sum = numbers.stream().reduce(0, Integer::sum);
```

For 3+ inputs, define a custom interface or use a record/tuple.

**Primitive specializations** — avoid boxing penalties:

| Generic | Primitive (int) | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `Function<Integer, Integer>` | `IntUnaryOperator` | int → int |
| `Function<Integer, R>` | `IntFunction<R>` | int → R |
| `Function<T, Integer>` | `ToIntFunction<T>` | T → int |
| `Predicate<Integer>` | `IntPredicate` | int → boolean |
| `Consumer<Integer>` | `IntConsumer` | int → void |
| `Supplier<Integer>` | `IntSupplier` | () → int |
| `BiFunction<Integer, Integer, R>` | `IntBiFunction` (custom) or use IntStream | |

Similar variants exist for `Long` (`LongFunction`, `ToLongFunction`, `LongPredicate`, etc.) and `Double`.

**When to use primitive variants**:
- Hot loops processing primitives.
- Stream pipelines: `.mapToInt(...)` + `.sum()` is faster than `.map(...)` + `.reduce(0, Integer::sum)`.
- Anywhere allocation pressure matters.

**Stream specialization**:
```java
// Boxed — each Integer is allocated
int sum = nums.stream().reduce(0, Integer::sum);

// Primitive — zero boxing in the pipeline
int sum2 = nums.stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).sum();
```

**Syntax**: `ClassName::staticMethod`

The simplest form. Refers to a `static` method of a class.

```java
Function<String, Integer> parser = Integer::parseInt;
// equivalent to: s -> Integer.parseInt(s)

Function<Object, String> toString = String::valueOf;
// equivalent to: o -> String.valueOf(o)

BiFunction<Integer, Integer, Integer> max = Math::max;
// equivalent to: (a, b) -> Math.max(a, b)

// In stream
List<Integer> ints = strings.stream()
    .map(Integer::parseInt)
    .toList();
```

**How it maps**:
- Lambda parameters → method arguments, in order.
- One-to-one: every lambda parameter is passed straight to the static method.
- Functional interface decided by context.

**Common static method references**:
- `Integer::parseInt`, `Integer::valueOf`, `Integer::sum`
- `Math::max`, `Math::min`, `Math::abs`
- `String::valueOf`
- `Objects::requireNonNull`, `Objects::isNull`, `Objects::nonNull`

**Syntax**: `instance::method` — the receiver is captured at lambda creation.

```java
String prefix = "Hello, ";
Function<String, String> greeter = prefix::concat;
// equivalent to: s -> prefix.concat(s)
// `prefix` (the String instance) is the receiver, `s` is the argument

List<User> users = ...;
Consumer<User> save = userRepo::save;
// equivalent to: user -> userRepo.save(user)
// `userRepo` is captured; each call invokes its save method

Consumer<String> print = System.out::println;
// `System.out` is captured; equivalent to: s -> System.out.println(s)
```

**Key point**: the receiver (`prefix`, `userRepo`, `System.out`) is **bound at the moment the method reference is created** — it's captured like an effectively-final variable.

**Capture implications**:
- The instance is captured — if it's a local variable, it must be effectively final.
- The instance is held by the lambda; it can't be garbage-collected until the lambda is.
- For per-call instances, a new method reference allocation may happen each time — minor cost, usually negligible.

**Common patterns**:
```java
// Spring service method as a Consumer
List<Order> orders = ...;
orders.forEach(orderProcessor::process);

// Cached helper
Map<String, User> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
Function<String, User> lookup = cache::get;  // s -> cache.get(s)

// Logger
List<Throwable> errors = ...;
errors.forEach(logger::error);
```

**Syntax**: `ClassName::instanceMethod` — the **first lambda parameter** becomes the receiver.

```java
Function<String, String> upper = String::toUpperCase;
// equivalent to: s -> s.toUpperCase()
// `s` is the receiver; the method is called ON it

Function<User, String> name = User::getName;
// equivalent to: u -> u.getName()

BiFunction<String, String, Boolean> equalsIgnoreCase = String::equalsIgnoreCase;
// equivalent to: (a, b) -> a.equalsIgnoreCase(b)
// First arg `a` is the receiver, `b` is the method argument

// In stream
List<String> upperNames = users.stream()
    .map(User::getName)        // unbound: u -> u.getName()
    .map(String::toUpperCase)  // unbound: s -> s.toUpperCase()
    .toList();
```

**The trick to remember**:
- **Bound** (`instance::method`): receiver is fixed at creation, lambda parameters become method arguments.
- **Unbound** (`Class::method`): first lambda parameter is the receiver, remaining parameters become method arguments.

```java
// Bound
String fixedPrefix = "X";
Function<String, String> bound = fixedPrefix::concat;
// bound.apply("abc") == "X".concat("abc") == "Xabc"

// Unbound
BiFunction<String, String, String> unbound = String::concat;
// unbound.apply("X", "abc") == "X".concat("abc") == "Xabc"
// First arg is the receiver, second is the argument
```

**Common in Comparator**:
```java
list.sort(Comparator.comparing(User::getName));  // unbound
```

**Syntax**: `ClassName::new` — refers to a constructor.

```java
Supplier<ArrayList<String>> listFactory = ArrayList::new;
// equivalent to: () -> new ArrayList<>()

Function<String, User> createUser = User::new;
// equivalent to: name -> new User(name)
// matches the constructor User(String)

BiFunction<String, String, User> createWithEmail = User::new;
// equivalent to: (name, email) -> new User(name, email)
// matches the constructor User(String, String)

// In stream — collect to a custom collection
LinkedList<String> linked = stream.collect(
    Collectors.toCollection(LinkedList::new));

// Mapping to objects
List<User> users = names.stream()
    .map(User::new)  // String -> User
    .toList();

// Array constructor reference
IntFunction<String[]> arrayCreator = String[]::new;
String[] arr = stream.toArray(String[]::new);
```

**Overload resolution** — if a class has multiple constructors, the compiler picks based on the target functional interface:
```java
class Point { Point() {}  Point(int x, int y) {} }

Supplier<Point> noArg = Point::new;          // matches Point()
BiFunction<Integer, Integer, Point> twoArg = Point::new;  // matches Point(int, int)
```

**Real-world**:
- `HashMap::new`, `ArrayList::new` for `Stream.collect(Collectors.toCollection(...))`.
- `User::new` for converting raw data (DTOs, rows) into domain objects.
- `String[]::new` (or `T[]::new`) for `stream.toArray(...)`.

The most common source of confusion is bound vs unbound instance references. They look similar but mean different things.

```java
String s = "hello";

// BOUND — receiver fixed at creation
Supplier<Integer> bound = s::length;
bound.get();  // returns s.length() == 5
// The instance `s` is captured; the lambda takes no arguments

// UNBOUND — receiver is the first lambda argument
Function<String, Integer> unbound = String::length;
unbound.apply("world");  // returns "world".length() == 5
// The lambda takes a String, calls length() on it
```

**How to spot the difference**:
- Looks like `instance::method` → **bound**, receiver is `instance`.
- Looks like `Class::method` and `method` is an instance method → **unbound**, first arg is receiver.
- Looks like `Class::method` and `method` is a static method → **static reference**.

**Compiler's job**: it disambiguates `Class::method` between static and unbound by checking if the method is `static` or `instance`. If both exist with the same name and matching signatures, the compiler picks based on the target functional interface.

**Edge case — same name, both static and instance**:
```java
class Util {
    public static String fmt(String s) { return s.trim(); }
    public String fmt() { return this.toString(); }
}
Function<String, String> staticRef = Util::fmt;  // static (matches Function<String, String>)
Function<Util, String> instanceRef = Util::fmt;  // unbound instance (matches Function<Util, String>)
```

**Use a method reference when**:
- The lambda just calls one method.
- The method name is clearer than the lambda body.
- You're calling a well-named method like `parseInt`, `getName`, `isEmpty`.

```java
// Cleaner with method reference
stream.map(User::getName)
      .filter(Objects::nonNull)
      .forEach(System.out::println);

// Versus more verbose lambda
stream.map(u -> u.getName())
      .filter(s -> s != null)
      .forEach(s -> System.out.println(s));
```

**Use a lambda when**:
- You need to rearrange parameters.
- You need to do more than one thing.
- You need to add a constant or transformation.
- The method-reference form is ambiguous (multiple overloads).

```java
// Lambda needed — parameter rearrangement
list.sort((a, b) -> b.compareTo(a));  // reversed
// Method reference would be Comparator.reverseOrder() instead

// Lambda needed — multiple operations
stream.map(u -> u.getName().toUpperCase());

// Lambda needed — constant addition
stream.map(u -> "User: " + u.getName());

// Lambda needed — ambiguous overload
stream.map(s -> Integer.valueOf(s));  // explicitly String version
// Integer::valueOf could match String, int, etc.
```

**Performance**:
- Method references are usually as fast or marginally faster than the equivalent lambda (one less synthetic method call).
- For non-capturing static method references, the call site is cached — zero allocation per call.
- Bound method references capture the receiver — one allocation per capture (small cost).
- For constructor references in hot paths, watch allocation; usually fine.

```java
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.*;

// SOURCES
List<Order> orders = ...;
orders.stream()                            // sequential — the default
orders.parallelStream()                     // parallel (use with care)
Stream.of("a", "b", "c")                    // explicit elements
IntStream.range(0, 100)                     // primitive range
Stream.iterate(0, n -> n + 1).limit(100)    // generator with bound
Files.lines(Path.of("log.txt"))             // I/O source (try-with-resources)

// INTERMEDIATE OPERATIONS
stream.filter(o -> o.getStatus() == COMPLETED)     // keep matching elements
      .map(Order::getAmount)                        // transform each element
      .flatMap(o -> o.getLineItems().stream())      // flatten nested streams
      .distinct()                                    // remove duplicates
      .sorted(Comparator.comparing(Order::getDate)) // sort (stateful!)
      .limit(100)                                    // cap at N
      .skip(10)                                      // drop first N
      .peek(o -> log.debug("processing {}", o));     // side-effect for debug

// TERMINAL OPERATIONS — trigger execution

// Collect to a List
List<Order> result = stream.collect(toList());
// Or Java 16+:
List<Order> result2 = stream.toList();

// Collect to a Map
Map<Long, Order> byId = orders.stream()
    .collect(toMap(Order::getId, Function.identity()));

// Group by key
Map<OrderStatus, List<Order>> byStatus = orders.stream()
    .collect(groupingBy(Order::getStatus));

// Group and reduce in one pass
Map<OrderStatus, BigDecimal> totalByStatus = orders.stream()
    .collect(groupingBy(Order::getStatus,
             reducing(BigDecimal.ZERO, Order::getAmount, BigDecimal::add)));

// Find
Optional<Order> first = orders.stream()
    .filter(o -> o.getAmount().compareTo(BigDecimal.valueOf(1000)) > 0)
    .findFirst();  // short-circuits

// Boolean queries
boolean anyLarge = orders.stream().anyMatch(o -> o.isLarge());
boolean allPaid = orders.stream().allMatch(Order::isPaid);
boolean noneFailed = orders.stream().noneMatch(Order::isFailed);

// Aggregate
long count = orders.stream().count();
Optional<Order> max = orders.stream()
    .max(Comparator.comparing(Order::getAmount));
BigDecimal total = orders.stream()
    .map(Order::getAmount)
    .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);

// Iterate (side-effect)
orders.forEach(o -> emailService.sendReceipt(o));

// To array
Order[] arr = orders.stream().toArray(Order[]::new);
```

| Usage | Verdict | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Return type on `findById`, `parse`, etc. | Correct | Forces caller to handle absence |
| Stream `findFirst`, `findAny`, `reduce` results | Correct | Designed by Stream API team |
| Field on JPA entity | Anti-pattern | Not Serializable; JPA doesn't support it |
| Method parameter | Anti-pattern | Caller must wrap/unwrap; use overloaded methods |
| Collection element type | Anti-pattern | Filter nulls first, or use Map for sparse data |
| Wrapping a primitive | Avoid | Use `OptionalInt`, `OptionalLong`, `OptionalDouble` to skip boxing |
| Nested (`Optional<Optional<T>>`) | Anti-pattern | Use `flatMap` to collapse |

**Pattern — overloaded methods instead of `Optional` parameters**:

```java
// ANTI-PATTERN
void logEvent(String message, Optional<String> context) { ... }
// Caller: logEvent("login", Optional.of("ip=1.2.3.4"));
//         logEvent("login", Optional.empty());

// CORRECT
void logEvent(String message) { logEvent(message, null); }
void logEvent(String message, String context) { ... }
// Caller: logEvent("login");
//         logEvent("login", "ip=1.2.3.4");
```

**Pattern — plain nullable fields on entities, `Optional` on repository method**:

```java
@Entity
class User {
    @Column String middleName;  // null is fine for a nullable column
    // NOT: Optional<String> middleName
}

interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
    Optional<User> findByEmail(String email);   // correct — absence possible
}
```

```java
// ANTI-PATTERN: isPresent + get is just null-check in disguise
Optional<User> opt = userRepo.findByEmail(email);
if (opt.isPresent()) {
    User u = opt.get();
    sendEmail(u);
} else {
    log.warn("User not found");
}

// IDIOMATIC: ifPresentOrElse (Java 9+)
userRepo.findByEmail(email)
    .ifPresentOrElse(
        this::sendEmail,
        () -> log.warn("User not found")
    );

// Transform — map
Optional<String> name = userRepo.findByEmail(email)
    .map(User::getName);

// Chain when transformation itself returns Optional — flatMap
Optional<Address> addr = userRepo.findByEmail(email)
    .flatMap(u -> addressRepo.findByUserId(u.getId()));

// Filter — refine
Optional<User> active = userRepo.findByEmail(email)
    .filter(User::isActive);

// Extract with default — literal only
String name = userRepo.findByEmail(email)
    .map(User::getName)
    .orElse("Anonymous");

// Throw when missing
User u = userRepo.findByEmail(email)
    .orElseThrow(() -> new NotFoundException("No user: " + email));

// Java 10+: throw default NoSuchElementException
User u2 = userRepo.findByEmail(email).orElseThrow();

// Lazy fallback chain (Java 9+)
Optional<User> found = primaryDb.find(id)
    .or(() -> secondaryDb.find(id))
    .or(() -> cache.find(id));

// Stream of Optionals → stream of present values (Java 9+)
List<User> users = emails.stream()
    .map(userRepo::findByEmail)
    .flatMap(Optional::stream)
    .toList();
```

This single distinction causes more production bugs than any other `Optional` misuse.

**`orElse(T)`** takes a value. The value is **always evaluated**, whether the Optional is present or not.

**`orElseGet(Supplier<T>)`** takes a supplier. The supplier is **only called** when the Optional is empty.

```java
// BAD — expensiveDefault() runs EVERY call, even when present
User user = userRepo.findByEmail(email)
    .orElse(expensiveDefault());

// GOOD — expensiveDefault() runs only when empty
User user = userRepo.findByEmail(email)
    .orElseGet(() -> expensiveDefault());
```

**Real-world impact** — an endpoint called 1000 times per second:

```java
public User getUser(String email) {
    return userRepo.findByEmail(email)
        .orElse(defaultUser());   // ALWAYS called, ALWAYS runs
}

private User defaultUser() {
    // Unknown to the original developer — this hits a remote service
    return remoteRegistry.getDefaultUser();   // 50ms RPC per call
}
```

Result: a 50ms remote call on every request whether needed or not. 50 seconds of extra work per second of clock time means the endpoint is 50x slower than it should be. I've traced this exact bug in production.

**The rule**:

- **`orElse`** for **cheap, literal** defaults: `orElse("")`, `orElse(0)`, `orElse(Collections.emptyList())`
- **`orElseGet`** for anything requiring computation — database lookups, object construction, remote calls

**Same principle** applies to `orElseThrow`:

```java
// GOOD — exception is only constructed when needed
User u = opt.orElseThrow(() -> new NotFoundException("id=" + id));

// BAD (and doesn't compile — orElseThrow takes a supplier, not a value)
// User u = opt.orElseThrow(new NotFoundException("id=" + id));
```

**Java 9+'s `or(Supplier<Optional<T>>)`** chains lazy fallbacks beautifully:

```java
Optional<User> u = primary.find(id)
    .or(() -> secondary.find(id))   // only called if primary empty
    .or(() -> cache.find(id));      // only called if both empty
```

Each fallback is invoked only if needed — no eager evaluation.
